WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.802
 Hey gang, Dave Baker.

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:04.036
Today we're going to talk about
some of my favorite pole arms

00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:05.305
featured on "Forged in Fire."

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:10.443
[music playing]

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.812
Historically, the pole
arm is a standoff weapon.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.513
It keeps your
enemy the distance.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.783
You fight off enemy cavalry
and infantry, keeping

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.285
them out of sword's range.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:27.626
Now this beauty is
the German halberd.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:30.363
German halberd was used
starting in the 14th century

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:33.966
consisting of an ax head, a
spear point, and a hooked back.

00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:35.735
It can be used to
chop an opponent,

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:39.039
pull someone off a horse,
or push a ladder off a wall.

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:43.576
 Whew.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:44.911
 It will kill.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.412
 Now the name of
this weapon, halberd,

00:00:46.478 --> 00:00:49.949
comes from the German words
halm, meaning staff, and bart,

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.051
meaning axe.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.056
 Sounds brutal, Dave.

00:00:57.156 --> 00:00:58.791
 You can still see
these halberds being

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:00.393
used at ceremonial events.

00:01:09.968 --> 00:01:11.403
 This is the glaive guisarme.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:15.541
Now a glaive is basically a
large sword blade on a pole.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:19.312
This is a widely used weapon
from the 13th and 14th century

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:20.412
in Europe.

00:01:20.512 --> 00:01:22.248
Now the glaive gave
the ability to thrust

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.550
as well as slash,
just like a pole.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.220
It can also be used to
push an object away.

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.461
 I'd say that's pretty good.

00:01:35.527 --> 00:01:37.863
 So glaives and weapons
like them were often

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:40.032
used by second line troops.

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:42.468
Standing behind the front
line or shield wall,

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:45.238
you could reach over and stab
into your opponent's line.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:49.875
 This weapon is sharp.

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:50.643
 Thank you.

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.120
 This weapon is
the qinglong ji.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:06.892
Now the qinglong ji
is almost a marrying

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.095
of a spear and a halberd.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.664
With this wavy blade for
thrusting and slashing,

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:14.567
the hook for either hooking or
chopping into an opponent, that

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.003
gave a warrior, a
certain amount of reach,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.672
but it wasn't as long
as a lot of pole arms.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.975
The qinglong ji was a
costly blade to make.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.844
So it was often reserved
for palace guards or the

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.679
upper echelon of the military.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:33.753
The qinglong ji was
a like, fast blade.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:36.955
And on the battlefield, if you
were facing a line of these,

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:37.924
you weren't moving forward.

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.269
This weapon is the bardiche.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:52.705
The bardiche is a Russian weapon
from the 16th, 17th century,

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:56.275
most famously used by a group
called the Streltsy, which

00:02:56.341 --> 00:02:58.411
were an elite musketeer group.

00:02:58.478 --> 00:03:00.846
Their heavy muskets were
laid across the axe's

00:03:00.946 --> 00:03:02.248
blade to stabilize it.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.450
Once the musket was
fired, it could be dropped

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:06.586
and the bardiche picked
up as troops charged.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.258
 Nice.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.525
 Right on, brother.

00:03:13.625 --> 00:03:15.895
 These are a light,
well-designed weapons.

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:17.363
Remember, on the
battlefield, you

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.166
need the carry this with one
hand and a 16-pound musket

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.333
in the other.

00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:24.103
That will really slow you down.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:25.271
 Oh, ha, ha.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.211
 It's a light, fast deadly
weapon with a colorful history.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.455
 This wild looking weapon
is the jumonji yari.

00:03:42.521 --> 00:03:46.692
The yari was used by Japanese
infantry in the 15th century

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.195
either to fight off
other infantry units

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.496
or mounted samurai.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.267
 Geezus.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:57.870
 The jumonji yari
or the trident yari

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.240
is different than the standard
yari in that the standard yari

00:04:01.306 --> 00:04:02.675
just has a point.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:05.077
The jumonji with these
arms actually made

00:04:05.143 --> 00:04:07.312
it a better defensive weapon.

00:04:07.412 --> 00:04:09.682
On the 15th century
Japanese battlefield,

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:13.253
this weapon was both
offense and defense.

